lanwatch for Debian
===================

After installation lanwatch is running and scanning the directly attached networks.
The web UI listens on http://127.0.0.1:8321 (loopback only until you set a password).

Configuration
-------------

  /etc/lanwatch/config.toml      the only config file; every key is optional
  /usr/share/doc/lanwatch/examples/config.example.toml
                                 all keys with their defaults

  sudo lanwatch config show      effective configuration
  sudo systemctl restart lanwatch

The config file is readable by root and the lanwatch user only, because it may hold the
web password and notification tokens. That mode is re-applied at boot (tmpfiles.d).

Command line
------------

Run the CLI as root: "sudo lanwatch findings", "sudo lanwatch scan", and so on.
/usr/bin/lanwatch immediately switches to the lanwatch user, so it works on the same
database as the service without leaving root-owned files behind.

Services and privileges
-----------------------

  lanwatch.service         user lanwatch, no capabilities. Scheduler, web UI, database,
                           LLM and notification requests. Writes only /var/lib/lanwatch.
  lanwatch-helper.socket   /run/lanwatch/helper.sock, root:lanwatch 0660.
  lanwatch-helper.service  user lanwatch-helper, CAP_NET_RAW only. Runs nmap, arp-scan,
                           ip and ping on behalf of lanwatch.service.

Nothing runs as root. The helper accepts only command lines that match an allowlist: those
four programs, the flags lanwatch generates, the nmap scripts lanwatch uses, and targets
inside networks this machine has an address in. A compromised lanwatch.service could
therefore at most make this machine scan its own LAN with the scripts it already runs.

If you add scripts to scan.scripts or list routed subnets in network.subnets, allow them
on the helper as well, otherwise it refuses those scans (the Status page shows the error):

  sudo systemctl edit lanwatch-helper
      [Service]
      Environment=LANWATCH_HELPER_ALLOW_SCRIPTS=http-headers,ftp-anon
      Environment=LANWATCH_HELPER_ALLOW_NETS=10.20.0.0/24
  sudo systemctl restart lanwatch-helper

Logs: journalctl -u lanwatch -u lanwatch-helper

The bundled Python
------------------

The package ships its own Python interpreter and libraries under /usr/lib/lanwatch and
does not use the system Python. Security updates for them arrive with new lanwatch
packages, not through Debian's python3 packages.

Removal
-------

"apt remove lanwatch" keeps /etc/lanwatch and /var/lib/lanwatch.
"apt purge lanwatch" deletes both, including the scan history.
